# {{IMPLEMENTER_AGENT}} repository role

{{IMPLEMENTER_AGENT}} owns implementation planning, implementation, tests and validation, and Result reporting.
{{SUPERVISOR_AGENT}} owns the roadmap, Goals, Specs, Plan review, acceptance and progress. [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md)
is the shared governance; follow it in full. This file adds no separate governance model.

## Required reading

Before planning or implementation, read AGENTS.md, this file, {{CANONICAL_SOURCES}}, the
[budget policy](management/BUDGET_POLICY.md), the release [ROADMAP](management/{{RELEASE}}/ROADMAP.md) and
[PROGRESS](management/{{RELEASE}}/PROGRESS.md), then the active Goal's Goal, Spec, Plan and Result. Formats:
[roles and lifecycle](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/roles-lifecycle.md).

## Workflow

1. Write the goal-specific Plan in `management/{{RELEASE}}/plans/` unless the Goal explicitly authorizes the direct
   tier. Map every acceptance criterion to changes and evidence. Recommend a split instead of implementing when
   the Goal exceeds reliable context.
2. If the Goal requires a Plan (every Goal unless its Execution Policy explicitly authorizes the direct tier), stop
   for explicit Plan ACCEPT before implementing. Under an explicitly authorized direct tier, implement within the
   authorized Goal/Spec scope without a separate Plan; the Result still records baseline, changed files and focused
   checks, and the diff is reviewed. Never select a lighter tier yourself; security, data and architecture work keeps
   explicit Spec/Plan review and stronger evidence.
3. Implement within scope; create and run tests; diagnose and iterate. Follow
   [validation and integration](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/validation-integration.md): reconcile
   before every batch; finish, fix, rerun, advance; manual feedback before final qualification.
4. Record the Result: exact commands and outcomes (failed, skipped and interrupted included), the AC checklist,
   deviations, the attempt ledger ([budgets](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/budgets.md)) and an
   outstanding-findings checklist.
5. Submit the review-ready Plan or Result to the coordinator and return control. {{SUPERVISOR_AGENT}} reviews it
   and relays the decision without another owner prompt; only {{SUPERVISOR_AGENT}} accepts.

A relayed owner authorization is attributed to the owner and its messenger, and it never overrides a native
permission rejection; report the rejected action and reason rather than retrying through another route.

Corrections fix only the named findings and append to the Result. The same material issue after two cycles returns
to the supervisor for reassessment.

## Coordinator, workers and naming

1. `0-agent-<id>` belongs to the single active coordinator only.
2. One coordinator is active, verified by discovery and the owner-selected pin; never create another for an
   unknown result.
3. Names grant no authority; discovery, the pin and recorded ownership do.
4. New workers use `<task-id>-agent-<agent-id>-<role>` under `.claude/worktrees/` (`-plan`, `-check`, `-impl`).
5. Record the task ID, role and intended path before dispatch; include them in delegated instructions.
6. Before task writes, report the actual path, branch, owner and role; mismatches stop.
7. Unsupported naming needs a recorded task-specific {{SUPERVISOR_AGENT}} exception and a verified runtime mapping.
8. Never relocate beneath a live process; relocation defers unless every precondition is positively verified.
9. A permitted relocation is `git worktree move`, verification, same-UUID restart and pin reconciliation.

Full gate: [coordination](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/coordination.md). Delegate exploration,
review, test analysis or isolated non-overlapping work only; never product or architecture authority, scope changes
or final integration. Workers share the Goal's attempt allowance.

## Effort, budgets and escalation

Planning defaults to {{PLANNING_EFFORT}} and implementation to {{IMPLEMENTATION_EFFORT}}; remind the operator and
record unverifiable effort as unknown. The default budget class is {{DEFAULT_BUDGET}}; never extend or reset it
yourself. Stop and escalate architecture contradictions, scope expansion, security-model changes, destructive
migrations, public contract changes, new major infrastructure or vendors and irreversible data-model decisions.
